Likes/Values/Psychographics: The “Likes” and “Dislikes” fields capture personal interests – this aligns with standard user-profile practice. In fact, modern user profiles often include psychographic data (interests, values, personality) to tailor experiences
userpilot.com
. Consider adding a “Core Values” or “Personal Goals/Mission” field to reflect what motivates the agent. (This is like a personal mission statement – it helps clarify values/goals for both the individual and team.)
Social/Accountability Links: You have Tribe/Squad and Mentor fields, which is great for social context. You could expand on social motivation: add a “Collaborators” or “Friends/Allies” list, or even a small leaderboard link. Studies show that ADHD users who work with others complete ~36% more tasks and feel less isolated
imaginovation.net
. Social features (team challenges, shared streaks, “study buddies”) reinforce accountability and can be built into the profile system.
Communication & Availability: It may help to include contact or status info – e.g. Slack/Discord handle, primary language, or time zone/working hours. Remote/team templates often note time zones to coordinate across regions. (No specific cite, but for a distributed empire this reduces confusion.) You could also add “Preferred Communication” (chat, video, email) to streamline interactions.
Reward/Progress Details: Your gamification fields (XP, level, tokens, badges) are on point. You might also display progress bars or streak counters visually. Research on gamified ADHD apps found that immediate rewards (points, badges, streaks) dramatically boost engagement
imaginovation.net
imaginovation.net
. For example, gamified apps saw 48% higher retention and 60% more task completion when they gave instant points/badges
imaginovation.net
. So ensure every small win (daily check-in, task done) yields XP or a badge to trigger dopamine motivation.
Missions & Quests: Consider a field for “Favourite Mission Type” or current “Quest”. This can gamify workflows: e.g. “Help onboard a new member” or “Complete 3 sprints today.” It makes the profile more dynamic. (This ties to the XP/Rewards; you could source quests from project trackers.)
Auto-Update / Live Dashboard: To make this card dynamic, hook it to real data. For instance, maintain the agent’s stats in a database or Notion/Google Sheet and regenerate the card automatically. Modern identity systems even support “automated credentials”: Ping Identity notes that credentials can pull data directly from a user’s profile so updates happen in real time
pingidentity.com
. In practice, you could write a script (in Python or JavaScript) to fill this Markdown template from an API or data store. Every time an agent’s XP or status changes, the script updates the card. This way, dashboards or “live profiles” stay in sync automatically
pingidentity.com
. (Tools like Notion, Glide, or an internal portal could host this.)
Technical Consistency: If this card is part of a broader system, consider standardized formats. For example, digital badges follow an “Open Badges” standard with metadata
